   "text": "This drawing is suramin, a symmetric polysulfonated urea. I read it in three parts and joined them.\n\n- **Terminal groups:** each end is a naphthalene bearing three sulfonic acids. The carbon carrying the amide NH is C8, and the SO3H groups sit at C1 (peri to the NH carbon), C3 and C5, giving an 8-aminonaphthalene-1,3,5-trisulfonic acid.\n- **Middle units:** each naphthylamine is acylated by a 4-methylbenzoyl ring. The methyl carbon is adjacent to the ring carbon bearing the second NH, and the carbonyl-bearing carbon is meta to that NH carbon. That second NH is acylated by a meta-substituted benzoyl ring.\n- **Centre:** the two meta-substituted benzoyl rings are joined through their ring carbons by a urea, NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH.\n\nSMILES: Cc1ccc(cc1NC(=O)c1cccc(NC(=O)Nc2cccc(c2)C(=O)Nc2cc(ccc2C)C(=O)Nc2ccc(S(=O)(=O)O)c3cc(S(=O)(=O)O)cc(S(=O)(=O)O)c23)c1)C(=O)Nc1ccc(S(=O)(=O)O)c2cc(S(=O)(=O)O)cc(S(=O)(=O)O)c12"
